course: geography
lesson: 55
title: Coasts and caves
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The coast is where land and sea fight, and in storms the waves hit cliffs with huge force, hurling pebbles and squeezing air into cracks.
explain: Where hard and soft rock lie side by side, the soft rock wears back into bays and the hard rock sticks out as headlands. Waves cut a cave into a headland, the cave breaks through to make an arch, and when the arch roof falls a lonely pillar is left: a stack.
explain: Watch the sand on a beach where waves come in at a slant: it is moved along the shore, step by step, by longshore drift, and can build a long finger of sand called a spit.
explain: Inland, caves are made in a different way: slightly acidic rain dissolves limestone. Drip by drip the water leaves stone behind, growing stalactites that hang down from the roof and stalagmites that grow up from the floor.
fact: Waves crash against coasts and wear them away, especially during storms.
fact: Coasts with hard and soft rock form headlands and bays, since soft rock wears back faster.
fact: Waves cut caves into headlands, the caves join into arches, and when arches fall, stacks are left.
fact: Australia's Twelve Apostles are limestone stacks that are slowly collapsing as the sea erodes them.
fact: Waves hitting a beach at an angle move sand along the coast, a process called longshore drift.
fact: A spit is a long finger of sand or shingle stretching out from the coast into the sea.
fact: The Holderness coast in Yorkshire is one of Europe's fastest-eroding coasts, losing about 1-2 metres a year.
fact: Most big caves form in limestone, which slightly acidic rain slowly dissolves.
fact: Stalactites hang from cave roofs and stalagmites grow up from the floor, built from dripping water.
fact: Rising seas caused by global warming make coastal erosion and flooding worse.
fact: The largest known cave passage is Son Doong in Vietnam, big enough to hold a city block with skyscrapers.
exercise: When do waves wear away coasts most? | During storms | On calm summer days | Only at night, in the dark | Only when the sea is frozen
exercise: Why does a coast of hard and soft rock get headlands and bays? | The soft rock wears back faster into bays | The hard rock wears back faster into bays | Waves only hit hard rock | Bays are dug out by people
exercise: What is the order as a headland is worn away? | Cave, then arch, then stack | Stack, then arch, then cave | Arch, then cave, then stack | Spit, then stack, then cave
exercise: What is a stack? | A pillar of rock left when an arch falls | A cave in the middle of a sandy beach | A pile of sand at a river mouth | A tall wave that rises up in a winter storm
exercise: What are the Twelve Apostles in Australia? | Limestone stacks slowly collapsing into the sea | Twelve tall mountains carved out by glaciers | Twelve caves full of stalactites | Twelve islands made by old volcanoes
exercise: What is longshore drift? | Waves moving sand along the coast when they hit at an angle | Sand blown inland by the wind to make tall dunes behind the beach | A cave forming in limestone | A wave so big it floods a town
exercise: What is a spit? | A long finger of sand or shingle reaching out to sea | A pillar of rock standing in the sea | A hole right through a headland | A pool left by the tide
exercise: About how fast is the Holderness coast in Yorkshire wearing away? | About 1-2 metres a year | About 1-2 centimetres a century | About 100 metres a day | It is growing, not eroding
exercise: In which rock do most big caves form? | Limestone | Hard granite | Black basalt | Grey slate
exercise: How do limestone caves form? | Slightly acidic rain slowly dissolves the rock | Hot lava tunnels melt their way through the rock | Bats dig them out | Earthquakes crack them open
exercise: Which hangs from a cave roof? | A stalactite | A stalagmite | A tall stack | A sandy spit
exercise: Which grows up from a cave floor? | A stalagmite | A stalactite | An arch | A headland
exercise: What builds stalactites and stalagmites? | Dripping water leaving stone behind | Ice freezing on the cold walls each winter | Bats' droppings piling up | Sand blown in by the wind
exercise: What makes coastal erosion and flooding worse today? | Rising seas caused by global warming | Colder winters in the tropics | More stalactites growing in sea caves | Fewer storms at sea
exercise: Where is Son Doong, the largest known cave passage? | Vietnam | Northern Iceland | Southern Australia | Northern Canada
test: little sis: "stalagMITES go up, stalacTITES come down — how do i remember?" which is right? | Stalactites hang down from the roof, stalagmites grow up | Stalactites grow up, stalagmites hang down | Both hang from the roof | Both grow from the floor
test: A seaside arch has a crack across its top after a winter of storms. What will it probably become one day? | A stack, once the top falls in | A cave, once it fills with sand | A spit, once it floats away | A bay, once it grows taller
test: A family's house on the Yorkshire coast is 20 metres from the cliff edge. Roughly when could the cliff reach it? | In about 10-20 years | In about 1,000 years or so | Next week, without fail | Never, cliffs do not move
test: A groyne, a low wooden wall across a beach, traps sand on one side only. Why? | Longshore drift moves sand along the beach and the wall stops it | The wall attracts sand like a magnet, but only on the side facing the sea | Sand only lands on the sunny side | The tide pushes sand inland only
test: "son doong's just a big hole in the ground, nothing special" — what's special about it? | It holds the largest known cave passage, big enough for skyscrapers | It is the deepest hole ever dug by people, deeper than any mine on Earth | It is a volcano crater full of sea water | It is a cave made of ice
test: A video game cave has stalactites made in one night by a dragon's breath. What is the real story? | They grow slowly from dripping water leaving stone | They are made by lava cooling quickly as it drips from the roof | They form from frozen bat breath | They are carved by miners
test: What would happen if a coast were made only of hard rock of one kind? | It would wear back evenly, with fewer headlands and bays | It would form many deep bays, one after another along the shore | It would never be touched by waves | It would turn into limestone
test: Why do sea levels rising with warming matter for coastal towns? | Erosion and flooding get worse | Beaches will grow much wider and safer | Cliffs will stop eroding for good | Storms will vanish
test: A long curved strip of sand sticks out into the sea at the end of a beach. What is it called? | A spit | A stack | A stalagmite | An arch
test: "caves r always made by waves, right" — Oli. Correct him. | Many big caves form inland, where rain dissolves limestone | Yes, every cave is made by waves, even ones far from the sea | No, caves are all dug by animals | No, caves are all made by earthquakes
test: Why do the Twelve Apostles not have twelve stacks standing today? | The sea erodes them and some have collapsed | Tourists broke bits off and took them home | They sank in an earthquake | They melted in the sun
exam: Identify the principal reason for which headlands and bays are formed along a coastline. | Less resistant rock is eroded more rapidly than resistant rock | Resistant rock is eroded more rapidly than less resistant rock | Waves exert no force upon soft rock | Bays are excavated exclusively by human activity
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sequence of features produced by the erosion of a headland? | Cave, arch and subsequently stack | Stack, arch and subsequently cave | Spit, bay and subsequently cave | Arch, spit and subsequently stack
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning longshore drift. | Sediment is conveyed along the coast by waves approaching at an angle | Sediment is conveyed inland by the wind exclusively | Sediment is dissolved by acidic rainfall | Sediment is deposited solely within caverns
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the majority of large caves are formed. | By the slow dissolution of limestone by slightly acidic rain | By the cooling of molten lava within mountains of volcanic origin | By the excavation of the ground by animals | By the impact of meteorites
